      B: { story: 'Role-based tokens: `--color-action-primary`, `--color-surface-background`, `--color-text-primary`. When the action colour changes from blue to green, only the token\'s value changes — its name remains correct. Developers always know what `--color-action-primary` means.', lesson: 'Token names should describe what something is for, not what it looks like. The appearance may change. The purpose is more stable.', principle: 'Token Naming', principleDetail: 'Semantic token naming: the name describes the design intent, not the visual value. This makes tokens resilient to rebrands, themes, and design changes.' },
      C: { story: 'A two-tier system: primitive tokens (`--blue-500: #3B82F6`) and semantic tokens (`--color-action-primary: var(--blue-500)`). When the action colour changes to green, you update one line: `--color-action-primary: var(--green-500)`. The primitive layer stays intact. The semantic layer is updated.', lesson: 'The two-tier approach is best practice: primitives define your palette, semantic tokens define its application. This is how Material Design, Polaris, and most mature design systems work.', principle: 'Token Naming', principleDetail: 'Tier 1 (primitive): --color-blue-500, --spacing-4, --font-size-body. Tier 2 (semantic): --color-action-primary references --color-blue-500, --spacing-card-padding references --spacing-4.' },
