    narration: "Launchly is adding dark mode. The engineering team asks: 'Do we need different tokens for dark mode or can we just invert all the colours?'\n\nYou're designing the token architecture for multi-theme support.",
    choices: [
      { id: 'A', text: 'Just invert the colours — dark mode is the opposite of light mode.', delta: -10 },
      { id: 'B', text: 'Keep the same semantic token names but map them to different values in dark mode. --color-surface maps to #FFFFFF in light, #1A1A2E in dark.', delta: 15 },
      { id: 'C', text: 'Create a separate set of dark-mode-specific tokens with different names for each theme.', delta: 5 },
    ],
    consequences: {
      A: { story: 'Colour inversion makes black text on white become white text on black. Fine. But it also makes your primary blue (#3B82F6) become its inverted complement (orange #F6BD3B). And your green success state becomes magenta. Inversion is not dark mode.', lesson: 'Dark mode is not colour inversion. Dark mode is a carefully chosen alternative colour palette that maintains the semantic meaning of each colour in a low-light context.', principle: 'Dark Mode Token Architecture', principleDetail: 'CSS `filter: invert()` or HSL inversion produces technically opposite colours that rarely match the design intent of a dark theme. Dark mode requires intentional colour choices per token, not mathematical transformations.' },
      B: { story: 'Semantic tokens maintain the same names across both themes. `--color-surface: #FFFFFF` in light mode becomes `--color-surface: #1E1E2E` in dark mode. The entire dark mode is applied by toggling a class on the root element that changes which token values are active.', lesson: 'Semantic tokens enable themability at zero added complexity. The same class names and component code work in both themes — only the token values change.', principle: 'Dark Mode Token Architecture', principleDetail: 'CSS class-based theming: .theme-light { --color-surface: #FFFFFF; } .theme-dark { --color-surface: #1E1E2E; }. Toggle the class on <html> to switch themes. Every component automatically updates because they reference tokens, not hardcoded values.' },
      C: { story: 'A separate set of dark-mode tokens (`--color-surface-dark`, `--color-primary-dark`) doubles the token count. Developers must remember to use the right token set for each theme. Components written for light mode forget to add dark mode token references. Inconsistency grows.', lesson: 'Theme-specific token names create parallel systems. Parallel systems require discipline to maintain and make errors easier to make.', principle: 'Dark Mode Token Architecture', principleDetail: 'The correct architecture: one set of semantic token names, two sets of values (one per theme). This is enforced by CSS specificity (the theme class overrides the default) rather than by developer discipline.' },
    },
    reflection: { law: 'Multi-theme Token Architecture', summary: 'Semantic tokens enable theming because you can change all values simultaneously by changing which token set is active. This is the architectural decision that makes dark mode cost 3 days instead of 3 weeks.', realWorld: "GitHub's Primer supports 6 colour modes (light, dark, light-high-contrast, dark-high-contrast, light-colorblind, dark-colorblind) with the same component code. This is possible because all colours reference semantic tokens, not hardcoded values.", bestCall: 'B — same semantic token names, different values per theme.', learnMore: 'Search: "dark mode token architecture" + "CSS custom properties theming"' },
